Robot's changes to espeak-ng (GPL-3.0-or-later), csukuangfj/espeak-ng at ed530aa113046142eb5115cf2fc9157854d0ffe1, the
fork sherpa-onnx v1.13.8 compiles in. Made for Robot on 31 Oct 2026, under the same licence. tools/build_sherpa.sh applies
it and tools/espeak_bounds_check.sh shows what it fixes.

Robot's own, in readclause.c's ReadClause:
- The embedded "B" command's list stays inside option_punctlist, and its writes into the clause buffer stop before the
  check at the end of the loop would have. The command "continue"s past that check, so a run of them wrote past the buffer.
- A punctuation announcement (up to 200 bytes, and only "B" turns them on) needs room in the clause buffer.

From espeak-ng/espeak-ng, the source changes of each commit named:
- 58f1e0b6 (CVE-2023-49990 to 49994), without its whitespace changes: RemoveEnding's word length, the empty phondata file,
  no empty voice name, wavegen's pitch, zeroed stack variables in SetWordStress and TranslateClause. Its "B" list bound
  is the first change above.
- 17ca9c9c: dotted abbreviations in LookupDictList and CheckDottedAbbrev, the words a dictionary entry skips, and the
  word count passed through the translation functions, so a long clause can't read past its word list.
- 210dcced: stacked suffixes in TranslateWord3.
- 8afe77ba, source files only: the start of the phoneme list passed down, so a condition can't read before it. Upstream
  starts it at the phoneme itself in WritePhMnemonic, which changes the IPA text voices are given (a "j" after some
  vowels); here it's phoneme_list, the list every caller walks, so the text stays what it was.

